.79999999999995,93.5535450709167 269.20000000000005,93.5535450709167 269.6,93.5535450709167 270,93.5535450709167 270.4,93.5535450709167 270.79999999999995,93.5535450709167 271.20000000000005,93.5535450709167 271.6,93.5535450709167 272,93.5535450709167 272.4,93.5535450709167 272.8,93.5535450709167 273.2,93.5535450709167 273.6,93.5535450709167 274,93.5535450709167 274.4,93.5535450709167 274.8,93.5535450709167 275.2,93.5535450709167 275.6,93.5535450709167 276,93.5535450709167 276.4,93.5535450709167 276.8,93.5535450709167 277.2,93.5535450709167 277.6,93.5535450709167 278,93.5535450709167 278.4,93.5535450709167 278.79999999999995,93.5535450709167 279.20000000000005,93.5535450709167 279.6,93.5535450709167 280,93.5535450709167 280.4,93.5535450709167 280.79999999999995,93.5535450709167 281.20000000000005,93.5535450709167 281.6,93.5535450709167 282,93.5535450709167 282.4,93.5535450709167 282.8,93.5535450709167 283.2,93.5535450709167 283.6,93.5535450709167 284,93.5535450709167 284.4,93.5535450709167 284.8,93.5535450709167 285.2,93.5535450709167 285.6,93.5535450709167 286,93.5535450709167 286.4,93.5535450709167 286.8,93.5535450709167 287.2,93.5535450709167 287.6,93.5535450709167 288,93.5535450709167 288.4,93.5535450709167 288.79999999999995,93.5535450709167 289.20000000000005,93.5535450709167 289.6,93.5535450709167 290,93.5535450709167 290.4,93.5535450709167 290.79999999999995,93.5535450709167 291.20000000000005,93.5535450709167 291.6,93.5535450709167 292,93.5535450709167 292.4,93.5535450709167 292.8,93.5535450709167 293.2,93.5535450709167 293.6,93.5535450709167 294,93.5535450709167 294.4,93.5535450709167 294.8,93.5535450709167 295.2,93.5535450709167 295.6,93.5535450709167 296,93.5535450709167 296.4,93.5535450709167 296.8,93.5535450709167 297.2,93.5535450709167 297.6,93.5535450709167 298,93.5535450709167 298.4,93.5535450709167 298.8,93.5535450709167 299.20000000000005,93.5535450709167 299.6,93.5535450709167 300,93.5535450709167\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"red\" stroke-width=\"1\"></polyline></svg><figcaption>Half of the area between <math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and the horizontal line <math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> lies above the line, and the other half lies below the line.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"Observe in the figure that the area below the curve ``y=f\\left(x\\right)`` and above the line ``y=f\\left(c\\right)`` is equal to the area above ``y=f\\left(x\\right)`` and below ``y=f\\left(c\\right)``. In this sense, ``f\\left(c\\right)`` is the *average value* of the function ``f\\left(x\\right)`` on the interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\n::: definition\nIf ``f`` is integrable on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then the *average value* or *mean value* of ``f`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, denoted by ``\\bar f``, is\n\n```math\n\\bar f = \\frac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^bf\\left(x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\\,.\n```\n:::\n\n### Piecewise Continuous Functions\n\nThe definition of integrability and the definite integral given above can be extended to a wider class than just continuous functions. One simple but very important extension is to the class of *piecewise continuous functions*.","output":"","result":"Observe in the figure that the area below the curve ``y=f\\left(x\\right)`` and above the line ``y=f\\left(c\\right)`` is equal to the area above ``y=f\\left(x\\right)`` and below ``y=f\\left(c\\right)``. In this sense, ``f\\left(c\\right)`` is the *average value* of the function ``f\\left(x\\right)`` on the interval ``\\left[a,b\\right]``.\n\n::: definition\nIf ``f`` is integrable on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then the *average value* or *mean value* of ``f`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, denoted by ``\\bar f``, is\n\n```math\n\\bar f = \\frac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^bf\\left(x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\\,.\n```\n:::\n\n### Piecewise Continuous Functions\n\nThe definition of integrability and the definite integral given above can be extended to a wider class than just continuous functions. One simple but very important extension is to the class of *piecewise continuous functions*.","resultHTML":"<p>Observe in the figure that the area below the curve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> and above the line <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=f\\left(c\\right)\\)</span> is equal to the area above <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> and below <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y=f\\left(c\\right)\\)</span>. In this sense, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(c\\right)\\)</span> is the <em>average value</em> of the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\left(x\\right)\\)</span> on the interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is integrable on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, then the <em>average value</em> or <em>mean value</em> of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, denoted by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\bar f\\)</span>, is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\bar f = \\frac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^bf\\left(x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n</div>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-ES111_061Lecture06-c3521-piecewise-continuous-functions\">\n<h1>Piecewise Continuous Functions</h1>\n<p>The definition of integrability and the definite integral given above can be extended to a wider class than just continuous functions. One simple but very important extension is to the class of <em>piecewise continuous functions</em>.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 50;\nconst xmid = 0.5 * scale;\nconst ymid = 2.5 * scale;\n\nconst a = 0.5;\nconst c1 = 1.5;\nconst c2 = 2.5;\nconst b = 3.5;\n\nconst f1 = (x) => Math.sqrt(1 - (x - 0.5) ** 2);\nconst f2 = () => 2;\nconst f3 = (x) => x - 2.5;\n\nconst segments = [\n    [f1, a, c1],\n    [f2, c1, c2],\n    [f3, c2, b],\n];\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 4.5 * scale,\n    height: 3 * scale,\n    caption: \"A piecewise continuous function.\",\n});\n\nconst points = [[xmid + a * scale, ymid]];\n\nfor (const [f, start, end] of segments) {\n    for (let i = 0; i <= 100; i++) {\n        const x = start + (end - start) * i / 100;\n        points.push([xmid + x * scale, ymid - f(x) * scale]);\n    }\n}\npoints.push([xmid + b * scale, ymid]);\n\nfigure.polygon()\n    .points(points.map(([px, py]) => `${px},${py}`).join(\" \"))\n    .fill(\"lightblue\")\n    .stroke(\"none\");\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [xmid, ymid],\n    scale,\n    x: {\n        ticks: [\n            { value: a, label: \"a\" },\n            { value: c1, label: \"c_1\" },\n            { value: c2, label: \"c_2\" },\n            { value: b, label: \"b\" },\n        ],\n    },\n    y: { ticks: [1, 2] },\n});\n\nfor (const [f, start, end] of segments) {\n    figure.plot(f, {\n        domain: [start, end],\n        samples: 101,\n        width: 1,\n        stroke: \"red\",\n    });\n}\n\nfor (const [x, y] of [\n    [a, f1(a)],\n    [c1, f1(c1)],\n    [c2, f2(c2)],\n    [b, f3(b)],\n]) {\n    figure.point(x, y, {\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: \"red\",\n        stroke: \"red\",\n    });\n}\n\nfor (const [x, y] of [\n    [c1, f2(c1)],\n    [c2, f3(c2)],\n]) {\n    figure.point(x, y, {\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: \"white\",\n        stroke: \"red\",\n    });\n}\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xyac1c2b12A piecewise continuous function.","result